markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Arrays in Objects

Now that you’ve looked at how objects group related information under a single name, it can be useful to examine how that same information can be organized using parallel arrays.

This provides a clear contrast between managing multiple separate data lists and the cleaner structure that objects offer.

---

<img src="https://images.code.org/8199feaed9f8c4f10129474b22065366-image-1765990868244.png" alt="example arrays" width="750px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

---

## Example

With parallel arrays, each array represents one property, and the values at the same index correspond to the same real-world item.

In the example above, index 0 in a **names** array, a **goals** array, and a **wcWin** array each provide a different detail about the same object.



<div style="text-align: center; width: 80%; margin: 0 auto;">
  <p style="
    font-size: 18px; 
    background-color: #F5F7F8; 
    padding: 10px; 
    border-left: 5px solid #8148B2; 
    border-right: 5px solid #8148B2;
    display: inline-block;
  ">
    Instead of keeping track of several arrays and ensuring their indexes stay synchronized, an object allows all related properties to live together in one place.
  </p>
</div>

---

## 🔍 Check for Understanding
